metallicity-index-law-89a7d43b.interpretation:
The formula 

 [Fe/H] = \log_{10} \left( $\frac{N_{Fe}}{N_H}$ \right)_{star} - \log_{10} \left( $\frac{N_{Fe}}{N_H}$ \right)_{sun}

 defines the metallicity index, 

 [Fe/H]

. The term 

 \left( $\frac{N_{Fe}}{N_H} \right)_{star}$

 represents the ratio of the number of iron atoms (

 N_{Fe}

) to hydrogen atoms (

 N_H

) found in the star being observed, typically derived from spectroscopic analysis. Similarly, 

 \left( $\frac{N_{Fe}}{N_H} \right)_{sun}$

 is the same ratio for the Sun, which serves as a universal reference standard. The use of 

 \log_{10}

 (base-10 logarithm) on both ratios converts them into a more manageable scale, as elemental abundances can span many orders of magnitude. By subtracting the Sun's logarithmic ratio from the star's, the formula effectively calculates the logarithm of the ratio of the star's iron-to-hydrogen ratio to the Sun's iron-to-hydrogen ratio (since 

 \log a - \log b = \log(a/b)

). A positive value of 

 [Fe/H]

 indicates that the star is more metal-rich than the Sun, while a negative value signifies that it is more metal-poor. A value of zero means the star has the same iron-to-hydrogen ratio as the Sun.

