spacelike-or-null-interval.symmetry_origin:
This concept originates from the Minkowski spacetime of special relativity, which unifies space and time into a four-dimensional continuum. The invariant spacetime interval 
$ds^2$
is a fundamental quantity that remains unchanged under Lorentz transformations, which are the symmetry transformations of spacetime. The classification of intervals into timelike (
$ds^2 > 0$
), null (
$ds^2 = 0$
), and spacelike (
$ds^2 < 0$
) is crucial for understanding causality in relativistic physics, defining which events can influence others.
